Raise the VideoFrameCompositor thread to QOS_USER_INTERACTIVE on OHOS.

HarmonyOS schedules threads by QoS level. base gives a kPresentation thread
its priority through a negative nice value, which an app cannot set, so the
renderer's VideoFrameCompositor thread ran at the default level -- mostly on
cores 0-3 of a Mate 70 Pro+. That thread converts and uploads every
software-decoded frame (6-12 ms for 1080p 10-bit to half-float textures,
growing as the phone warmed) and also answers the display's begin-frames
for video. ohos-video-render-diagnostics.patch showed YouTube's 1080p60 HDR
with a frame always ready when Render() ran, yet a third of the frames
dropped, more as the upload slowed: the begin-frames came late.

On OHOS the thread raises itself to QOS_USER_INTERACTIVE as its first task,
through libqos.so opened at run time, and logs whether that worked.

Generated against Chromium 154.0.8037.51.

Apply with:
  git -C /path/to/chromium/src apply /path/to/patches/ohos-video-compositor-qos.patch

diff --git a/content/renderer/renderer_blink_platform_impl.cc b/content/renderer/renderer_blink_platform_impl.cc
index 4341f40..9117605 100644
--- a/content/renderer/renderer_blink_platform_impl.cc
+++ b/content/renderer/renderer_blink_platform_impl.cc
@@ -33,6 +33,10 @@
 #include "base/time/time.h"
 #include "base/time/time_delta_from_string.h"
 #include "build/build_config.h"
+
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+#include <dlfcn.h>
+#endif
 #include "cc/base/features.h"
 #include "components/content_settings/core/common/content_settings_pattern.h"
 #include "components/input/features.h"
@@ -153,6 +157,31 @@ namespace content {
 
 namespace {
 
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+// HarmonyOS schedules threads by QoS level. base sets a kPresentation
+// thread's priority through a negative nice value, which an app cannot set
+// here, so the VideoFrameCompositor thread ran at the default level -- on
+// a Mate 70 Pro+ mostly on cores 0-3 -- and the 10-bit conversion and
+// upload of each software-decoded frame it does took 6-12 ms. The same
+// thread answers the display's begin-frames for video, and the jitter that
+// added dropped a third of YouTube's 1080p60 HDR frames with the decoder
+// keeping up. Raise it to QOS_USER_INTERACTIVE; libqos.so is opened rather
+// than linked so a system without it only loses the raise.
+void RaiseVideoFrameCompositorQoS() {
+  // QoS_Level's QOS_USER_INTERACTIVE, from <qos/qos.h>.
+  constexpr int kQoSUserInteractive = 5;
+  void* library = dlopen("libqos.so", RTLD_NOW | RTLD_LOCAL);
+  auto* set_qos = library ? reinterpret_cast<int (*)(int)>(
+                                dlsym(library, "OH_QoS_SetThreadQoS"))
+                          : nullptr;
+  const int result = set_qos ? set_qos(kQoSUserInteractive) : -1;
+  LOG(WARNING) << "OHOS VideoFrameCompositor thread QoS: "
+               << (!set_qos ? "unavailable"
+                   : result == 0 ? "user interactive"
+                                 : "refused");
+}
+#endif  // BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+
 // TODO(crbug.com/40550966): Move this method and its callers to Blink.
 media::AudioParameters GetAudioHardwareParams() {
   blink::WebLocalFrame* const web_frame =
@@ -1159,6 +1188,10 @@ RendererBlinkPlatformImpl::VideoFrameCompositorTaskRunner() {
         std::make_unique<base::Thread>("VideoFrameCompositor");
     video_frame_compositor_thread_->StartWithOptions(
         base::Thread::Options(base::ThreadType::kPresentation));
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+    video_frame_compositor_thread_->task_runner()->PostTask(
+        FROM_HERE, base::BindOnce(&RaiseVideoFrameCompositorQoS));
+#endif
   }
 
   return video_frame_compositor_thread_->task_runner();
